# DURABLE REPRO — RETURNING a `Guard[T]` from a function does not compile: the
# return-position ownership boundary emits a call to `gorget_guard_clone`, a
# runtime symbol that does not exist.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg):
#   `gg check`                -> "OK: no semantic errors"
#   `gg build`                -> C, in the emitted `take`:
#        error: implicit declaration of function 'gorget_guard_clone';
#               did you mean 'gorget_map_clone'?
#        error: incompatible types when assigning to type 'Guard__Inner'
#               {aka 'gorget_guard_t'} from type 'int'
#      (the second error is a CONSEQUENCE of the first — C gives an implicitly
#      declared function an `int` return type.)
#   `gg build --backend=llvm` -> builds the IR, then the LINK fails:
#        undefined reference to `gorget_guard_clone'
#      ⚠ MIND THE PROBE: the LLVM lane needs the LINK step to see this — an
#      IR-only check reads as clean.
#
# ⚠ NO FIELD ACCESS IS INVOLVED. This fixture never reads `.fd` inside `take`,
# and the shape still fails, which is what makes it a DISTINCT defect from the
# guard field-access family (`known_gaps/sound_guard_param_field_unsafe.gg`,
# whose bare-param row also surfaces `gorget_guard_clone` but only when a
# MUTATING field access forces a CoW materialize). Here the clone is demanded by
# the RETURN boundary alone.
#
# SCOPE — one cell: `Guard[T]` at a RETURN position, out of a bare-parameter
# `Mutex[T]`. Not sampled here: `!`/`&` parameter spellings, `ReadGuard`,
# `WriteGuard`, and returning a guard out of a method rather than a free
# function.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): a guard is a single-owner value; returning one
# MOVES it to the caller (no clone is required or meaningful — a guard has no
# clone path by design, per the single-owner carve-out in AGENTS.md's
# "Ownership at Consuming Positions"). The program prints `10`.
# ⚠ So the reference-grade fix is almost certainly NOT to add a
# `gorget_guard_clone` runtime function — it is to stop demanding a clone at a
# boundary a move satisfies.
#
# ⚠ THIS FIXTURE CANNOT RUN UNTIL FIXED: no binary is produced on either lane.
#
# The seed is COMPUTED, not a literal, so no const-fold can elide the guard.
#
# Cited from TODO.md (guard/wrapper subsystem).
struct Inner:
    int fd

int seed():
    return 10

Guard[Inner] take(Mutex[Inner] m):
    return m.lock()

void main():
    Mutex[Inner] m = Mutex[Inner](Inner(seed()))
    Guard[Inner] g = take(m)
    print(g.fd)
